# SKILL_GRAPH — {{PROJECT_NAME}}

> **Purpose.** The living graph of *skills*: procedures this project has learned,
> written so precisely that a small, low-capability agent following one can
> reproduce the result a frontier model achieved. SCARS.md records what NOT to
> do; a skill records **how to do it right, step by step, with checks**.
>
> This file is where the project's INTELLIGENCE is stored — not the model's
> weights, not chat history: distilled reasoning as executable procedure.
> Discovery is paid once, by whoever is smartest that day; execution is cheap
> forever, by whoever is available — any runtime, any vendor, offline included.
>
> **Contract for every agent (any runtime).**
> 1. Before building something, SEARCH this graph. If a skill exists, follow it
>    and run its VERIFYs. Do not rediscover.
> 2. When you learn a reusable procedure (≥30 min of discovery, or any solved
>    bug/issue another agent could hit), write or extend a skill under
>    [`.agents/skill/{{PROJECT_SLUG}}/skills/`](.agents/skill/{{PROJECT_SLUG}}/skills/)
>    and register it here **in the same commit**.
> 3. Update `updated:` on every substantive edit. Never delete a skill; mark it
>    `status: superseded` and link its replacement.
>
> **Quality bar — the 1B-model test.** A skill is complete only if an agent with
> minimal reasoning can execute it: every step is a concrete command or click
> path, every branch an explicit IF/THEN, every step states its VERIFY check,
> and failure modes link to SCARS anchors.
>
> **Certification — measured, never asserted.** `certified:` lists ONLY model
> tiers that actually executed the skill end-to-end with its eval passing
> (a name is a claim). A failing eval flips `status: broken` — loudly, never
> silently wrong.

