## Evals

Every `active` skill has an executable eval beside it
(`skills/<name>.eval.sh` or an `## Eval` block: setup → execute the skill's
steps → assert the outcome). Run one: `atlas skill test <name>` (or execute the
file). Record each certification run: date, model tier, pass/fail, cost —
that record is what backs the `certified:` column, and the cost delta vs the
original discovery is the skill's measured ROI.

## Federation (optional)

This graph can be exported (skills/scars/decisions as typed, content-addressed
nodes with provenance: repo, commit, eval status) and merged into a global
personal/org knowledge graph. Markdown is the source of truth; any database is
a deterministic, rebuildable index. Promotion to a shared graph requires:
provenance complete + eval green + a recorded review — a skill is executable
instruction, so an unreviewed skill from elsewhere is treated as untrusted
input, never followed blindly.

## How to add a skill (held to its own bar)

1. Create `.agents/skill/{{PROJECT_SLUG}}/skills/<kebab-name>.md` with
   frontmatter `name / status: active|draft / certified: [] / created /
   updated / owner: any-agent`, then sections: **When to use · Prerequisites ·
   Decision tree · Procedures (numbered; each step = action + VERIFY) ·
   Failure modes (→ SCARS links) · Eval · Sources**.
2. Write the eval; run it; record the run.
3. Add one Registry row and the graph edges it earns. Same commit.
4. VERIFY: another agent (ideally a smaller model) executes the skill from the
   file alone. Whatever tier succeeds is what `certified:` may claim.
